<BaseLayout title="FM Radio Kit with Bluetooth Control" description="Open source FM radio kit built around the RDA5807M chip and the ScoutMakes Azul BLE board, with CircuitPython support and RDS.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Kit · OSHWA US000675</p>
  <h1>ScoutMakes FM Radio Kit with Bluetooth Control</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A classic build for anyone getting started in DIY electronics — an engaging exploration of FM radio,
    with Bluetooth control from across the room.
  </p>

  <p>
    Building an FM receiver from scratch usually means juggling resistors, capacitors, transistors, and an
    amplifier. Instead, this kit is built around a single integrated chip — the RDA5807M, a broadcast FM stereo
    tuner with a fully integrated synthesizer and a low-IF digital audio processor — on a PCB that complies with
    the Feather and STEMMA standards, programmable via a CircuitPython library.
  </p>

  <p>
    The kit also receives and decodes Radio Data Service (RDS) content — the same protocol that shows song and
    station names on a modern car stereo.
  </p>

  <ul class="spec-list">
    <li>Open hardware and open source software</li>
    <li>RDA5807M single-chip FM receiver</li>
    <li>Controllable over BLE using an iOS or Android app</li>
    <li>Portable, powered by a rechargeable LiPo battery</li>
    <li>High-quality stereo audio output with volume and bass control</li>
    <li>Received signal strength indicator (RSSI) data</li>
    <li>Station scanning and presets supported in code</li>
    <li>RDS information available for display</li>
    <li>Adafruit CircuitPython library</li>
    <li>User-assignable push-buttons for control</li>
    <li>3.5&nbsp;mm audio jack</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <a href="/learn/fm-radio-kit-assembly">Assembly instructions</a> and design files walk through building the
    kit and using the Bluefruit Connect app for wireless control.
  </p>

